#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097530} :activity/id #uuid "5599d88c-65b1-5f17-bfd1-cbd036adf9c2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048281 :topic/id #uuid "90162f1c-f68e-578c-b674-1f62c53b596b" :topic/math-academy-id 1141} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097530 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097928 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x^3e^{x/2} \\textrm{d}x$. We can do it by applying integration by parts three times, but the calculation is pretty long. How can we speed it up?\n\nMultiple applications of integration by parts becomes quick and easy if we use the **tabular method**.\n\nLet's start by picking our $u$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nas normal:\n\n$$\nu = x^3\\,,\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^{x/2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe put this in a table, as shown below:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n\nTo fill in the second row of the table, we differentiate the function in the first column and integrate the function in the second column. Doing this, we find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(x^3\\right) = 3x^2\\,,\\quad \\int e^{x/2} \\textrm{d}x = 2e^{x/2}\\,\n$$\n\nFilling in our table, we get the following:\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ |  |\n\nAt this step, we begin filling the third column. It always starts with a \"$+$\" and then the signs alternate at each subsequent step.\n\nWe continue by differentiating the expression in the first column and integrating the expression in the second column until we get zero in the first column.\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 6x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 4e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0.5,0}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 6$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 8e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}16e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}$ |\n\nNow we multiply the same colored terms along with the same colored sign. Specifically we multiply $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ with ${\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} 2e^{x/2}}$, $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}3x^2$ with $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}4e^{x/2}$, and so on. We get\n\n| **Derivatives** | **Integrals** | **Sign** | **Same Color Products** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} x^3$ | $e^{x/2}$ |  |  |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06}3x^2$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0}2e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,0} 2x^3e^{x/2}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 6x$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} 4e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{0.77,0.45,0.06} -12x^2e^{x/2}$ |\n| $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} 6$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 8e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} +$ | $\\color[rgb]{0,0,1} 48xe^{x/2}$ |\n| $0$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1}16e^{x/2}$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -$ | $\\color[rgb]{1,0,1} -96e^{x/2}$ |\n\nFinally, we add up all the terms of the last column to get the answer for our integral. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intx^{3}e^{x/2}dx &= 2x^{3}e^{x/2} - 12x^{2}e^{x/2} + 48xe^{x/2} - 96e^{x/2} + C \\\\\n&= e^{x/2}(2x^{3} - 12x^{2} + 48x - 96) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ce8fcadc-c0d9-53c8-93f1-7c7946d0ec31" :tutorial/math-academy-id 907 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a907607b-33d6-5787-9f76-4ba49a6d4daf" :step/math-academy-id 1760 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097531}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097531 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096680 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096479 :question/id #uuid "f4bca758-9903-505c-9828-0e1788b25d9f" :question/math-academy-id "e-499"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ffce8b3-cc12-5a4d-88fd-77889b6bdd3f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097062}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using the Tabular Method of Integration by Parts"} :step/id #uuid "2713bc66-d154-57b0-a43a-a25388531743" :step/math-academy-id 1761 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097532}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097532 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096681 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096482 :question/id #uuid "96f1c791-f1cd-5a20-a6ee-004475bbd482" :question/math-academy-id "e-500"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "708faaf1-be43-502c-88dc-94270e8783b6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097168}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Algebraic and Exponential Expressions Using the Tabular Method of Integration by Parts"} :step/id #uuid "ba6ae8dc-2771-5af3-981d-bd894fac5705" :step/math-academy-id 1765}] :activity/title "The Tabular Method of Integration by Parts" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096911}]}
